'''''Ornithogalum broteroi''''', a [[species]] of the [[genus]] ''[[Ornithogalum]]'', is a [[perennial]] [[bulbous]] [[flowering plant]] in the asparagus family ([[Asparagaceae]]). It is classed in the ''Cathissa'' group of the genus. It bears white flowers and usually a single leaf. It is found in open woods and pastures in the western part of the Iberian Peninsula and also Morocco.

== Description ==

''Ornithogalum broteroi'' shares a number of features with the other two members of the ''Cathissa'' group, namely [[spicate]] to subspicate (spiked) [[inflorescence]]s and [[Corolla (flower)|corolla segments]] that lack conspicuous green stripes on the reverse surface.{{sfn|Martínez-Azorín et al|2006}}{{sfn|Tutin et al|1980}}

''O. broteroi'' is characterised by [[Scape (botany)|scapes]] that have a single leaf, which are usually single but occasionally two–three. The plant arises from a bulb, that is about 8–12&nbsp;mm in diametre, and reaches a height of {{Convert abbreviated|2-10|cm|4=1}}, occasionally up to {{Convert abbreviated|25|cm}}.{{sfn|Martínez-Azorín et al|2006}}{{sfn|Tutin et al|1980}}

The [[leaves]], which are {{Convert abbreviated|5-15|cm}} in length and 2–6.5&nbsp;mm wide, are [[lanceolate]] and [[Glabrous (botany)|glabrous]] with a wide sheath at the base, then tapering to an appendage that is [[cuspidate]] and cylindrical and nearly as long as the leaf blade. The short pedicels are 1.5–3&nbsp;mm (but may be up to 4&nbsp;mm) long, while the much longer [[bract]]s are 8–10&nbsp;mm long. The inflorescence typically has 3-5 (sometimes 8) [[flower]]s, and has [[perianth]] segments that are 10–16&nbsp;mm long and white. The [[Ovary (botany)|ovary]], which is rounded at the apex (obovate-lanceolate to obtusely truncate apex) is up to two times longer than it is wide, and the [[Stigma (botany)|style]] is longer than it. The [[fruit]] is a [[Capsule (fruit)|capsule]], oblong to ovoid in shape. The [[seed]]s, which are 1.6–1.9&nbsp;mm long are [[subglobose]], with one pointed edge, and have a seed coat that is made up of numerous irregular pieces delimited by ridges.{{sfn|Martínez-Azorín et al|2006}}{{sfn|Tutin et al|1980}}

[[Chromosome number]]: 2n=34{{sfn|Martínez-Azorín et al|2006}}{{sfn|Tutin et al|1980}}

== Taxonomy ==

''Ornithogalum broteroi'' has a complicated history. Known at least, to [[Theophrastus]] and [[Carolus Clusius|Clusius]], the species was first formally described by [[Carl Linnaeus]] in 1753, as ''Scilla unifolium'', one of eight ''Scilla'' species. This was his name for a plant from Portugal previously known as ''Bulbus monophphyllus, flore albo''.{{sfn|Bauhin|Cherler|1650–1651}}{{sfn|Linnaeus|1753}} Subsequently (1799) [[Johann Heinrich Friedrich Link|Link]] reclassified it in the related genus ''Ornithogalum'', as ''O.&nbsp;unifolium'',{{sfn|Link|1799|p=320}} which was an [[illegitimate combination]]. It was fully described and illustrated under that name in 1806 by [[John Bellenden Ker Gawler|Ker Gawler]] (two specimens) in [[John Sims (taxonomist)|Sims]]' Botanical Magazine,{{sfn|Sims|1806a}}{{sfn|Sims|1806b}} and it is still referred to by that name in some texts.{{sfn|Tutin et al|1980}} The Portuguese botanist [[Félix de Avelar Brotero]] had also renamed it as ''O.&nbsp;nanum'', but this too was shown to be illegitimate, there being pre-existing [[homonym]]s for both, and also the alternative ''O.&nbsp;spicatum'' <small>Planellas</small>.{{sfn|Brotero|1804}} the only legitimate name is ''Ornithogalum broteroi'', proposed by M. Lainz in 1971,{{sfn|Lainz|1971}} and consequently bears his name as the [[botanical authority]].{{sfn|POWO|2022}}{{sfn|Martínez-Azorín et al|2006}}

The infrageneric relationships (and hence the phylogeny) of Ornithogalum has been controversial.{{sfn|Martinez-Azorin ''et al.''|2011}} Briefly The ''sensu stricto'' classification of {{harvp|Martinez-Azorin ''et al.'' |2011}} reduces the number of species to 50 as originally proposed by Speta.{{sfn|Speta|1998}} Thus, any consideration of the genus needs to be examined as to whether it refers to ''sensu stricto'', the 50 species considered by {{harvp|Speta|1998}} and {{harvp|Martinez-Azorin ''et al.'' |2011}}, or ''sensu lato'', the much larger genus envisaged by {{harvp|Manning ''et al.'' |2009}}. Under the ''sensu stricto'' construction, ''O.&nbsp;broteroi'' and two other species (''[[Ornithogalum concinnum|O. concinnum]]'' <small>Salisb.</small> and ''[[Ornithogalum reverchonii|O. reverchonii]]'' <small>Lange</small>) are [[Segregate (taxonomy)|segregated]] to a separate genus, ''Cathissa'' <small>[[Richard Anthony Salisbury|Salisb.]]</small> and is designated ''Cathissa broteroi'' <small>(M.Laínz) Speta</small>. Under the ''sensu lato'' construction ''Cathissa'' is treated as a subgenus of ''Ornithogalum'' (subg. ''Cathissa'' <small>(Salisb.) [[John Gilbert Baker|Baker]]</small>.{{sfn|Martinez-Azorin ''et al.'' |2011}}

=== Etymology ===

The [[specific epithet]] ''broteroi'' recognises the contribution of Félix de Avelar Brotero.

== Distribution and habitat ==

[[Littoral zone|Littoral]] areas of the Western [[Iberian Peninsula]], from northern [[Cabo de Finisterre]], and along the coast of Portugal to Gibraltar and in some parts inland to the Spanish border and northwestern Morocco to [[Marrakesh|Marrakech]]. It is found in open woods and pastures.{{sfn|Martínez-Azorín et al|2006}}{{sfn|Tutin et al|1980}}{{sfn|POWO|2022}}

== Ecology ==

Flowering time is February to March.{{sfn|Tutin et al|1980}}

